# Objective

The objective of this research is to identify the most suitable battery technology for the Chronis AI wearable device. The selected battery should provide sufficient runtime while maintaining a compact form factor, high safety standards, and reliable performance under varying workloads such as continuous sensing, periodic image capture, audio recording, and wireless communication.

---

# Battery Technologies Studied

## 1. Lithium Polymer (Li-Po)

### Overview
Lithium Polymer batteries are lightweight rechargeable batteries that use a polymer electrolyte instead of a liquid electrolyte. Due to their thin profile and high energy density, they are the preferred choice for modern wearable devices.

### Advantages
- High energy density
- Very lightweight
- Thin and flexible form factor
- Fast charging capability
- High discharge current support
- Excellent for compact wearable electronics

### Disadvantages
- Sensitive to physical damage
- Requires protection circuitry
- Slightly shorter cycle life than LiFePO₄

### Suitability
★★★★★ (Highly Recommended)

---

## 2. Lithium-Ion (Li-Ion)

### Overview

Lithium-Ion batteries are commonly used in smartphones, laptops, and portable electronics. They provide high capacity but are generally cylindrical or rigid in shape.

### Advantages

- High capacity
- Good energy density
- Long operating time
- Mature technology

### Disadvantages

- Rigid form factor
- Less suitable for compact wearables
- Requires protection circuit

### Suitability

★★★★☆

---

## 3. Lithium Iron Phosphate (LiFePO₄)

### Overview

LiFePO₄ batteries are well known for their excellent safety and extremely long cycle life.

### Advantages

- Excellent thermal stability
- Very safe chemistry
- Long cycle life (2000–5000 cycles)
- Stable voltage output

### Disadvantages

- Lower energy density
- Larger and heavier
- Not ideal for thin wearable devices

### Suitability

★★★☆☆

